= 1H
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=1H}

Write $n=2^r m$ with $m$ odd. If $m>1$, the <polynomial factor> of $X^m+1$ by $X+1$, evaluated at $X=10^{2^r}$, supplies a proper divisor. Therefore **$n$ must be a power of two**. Conversely this is only a necessary condition for <primality testing>.

For a <prime factor> $p$ of the number, $p$ is odd and does not divide $10$. Its <multiplicative order> $d$ satisfies $d\mid 2n$ but $d\nmid n$, since $10^n\equiv-1\pmod p$. When $n$ is a power of two, every proper divisor of $2n$ divides $n$, so $d=2n$. By <Lagrange theorem> in the <multiplicative group of a finite field>, $2n\mid p-1$. Thus $\boxed{p\equiv1\pmod{2n}}$.

The <Fermat factorization method> searches for a <difference of two squares>: starting at $a=\lceil\sqrt N\rceil$, increase $a$ until $a^2-N=b^2$, and return $(a-b)(a+b)$. Every factorization of an odd integer has this form, with $a=(d+e)/2$ and $b=(e-d)/2$. For $N=10001$, the successive differences at $a=101,102,103,104,105$ are $200,403,608,815,1024$. The last is $32^2$, giving $\boxed{10001=73\cdot137}$. Trial division by the <primes> up to their respective square roots confirms that both factors are <prime>.

= 2I
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=2I}

For nonempty <compact sets>, define the <distance from a point to a closed set> by $d(x,B)=\min_{b\in B}|x-b|$ and the <Hausdorff distance> by
$$
d_H(A,B)=\max\{\sup_{a\in A}d(a,B),\sup_{b\in B}d(b,A)\}.
$$
The minima and suprema exist: the sets are nonempty and <compact>, and the distance function is <Lipschitz continuous>. The <Hausdorff distance> is finite, nonnegative and symmetric. If it is zero, every point of $A$ belongs to the <closed set> $B$ and conversely, so $A=B$; the converse is immediate.

For $a\in A$, choose $b\in B$ attaining $d(a,B)$ and then $c\in C$ attaining $d(b,C)$. The <triangle inequality> gives $d(a,C)\leq d(a,B)+d_H(B,C)$. Taking the supremum over $a$, and doing the same with $A,C$ exchanged, proves $d_H(A,C)\leq d_H(A,B)+d_H(B,C)$. Hence this is a <metric>.

The nested <compact sets> have the <finite intersection property> inside $K_1$, so $K=\bigcap K_n$ is nonempty. It is a <closed subset> of $K_1$ and therefore <compact>. Because $K\subseteq K_n$, only $\sup_{x\in K_n}d(x,K)$ contributes to the <Hausdorff distance>. If this did not tend to zero, some increasing indices $n_j$ and $x_j\in K_{n_j}$ would satisfy $d(x_j,K)\geq\epsilon>0$. A <convergent subsequence> in $K_1$ has limit $x$. For each fixed $m$, its tail belongs to the <closed set> $K_m$, so $x\in K$. But then $d(x_j,K)\leq|x_j-x|\to0$, a contradiction. Thus $\boxed{d_H(K_n,K)\to0}$.

= 3G
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=3G}

Work over the <finite field> $\mathbb F_2$. Reduce <polynomials> modulo $X_i^2-X_i$, so their monomials are square-free. The binary <Reed-Muller code> $\operatorname{RM}(r,m)$ consists of the evaluation vectors of <polynomials> of degree at most $r$ at all $2^m$ points of $\mathbb F_2^m$, in a fixed order. This is a <linear code> of length $2^m$ and dimension $\sum_{j=0}^r\binom mj$, for $0\leq r\leq m$. Square-free monomials give independent evaluation functions, as follows inductively by splitting according to the last variable.

The <minimum Hamming distance of a linear code> equals its least nonzero <Hamming weight>. The result is
$$
\boxed{d(\operatorname{RM}(r,m))=2^{m-r}\quad(0\leq r\leq m).}
$$
Here $r=0$ is the <repetition code>, while $r=m$ is the full binary vector space and has distance one. For the induction step, write $f(x,t)=u(x)+t v(x)$, where $\deg u\leq r$ and $\deg v\leq r-1$. Its evaluation vector is $(u,u+v)$, the <Reed-Muller bar-product recursion>. If $v=0$, its nonzero <Hamming weight> is at least $2d(r,m-1)=2^{m-r}$, with the full-space endpoint treated as above. If $v\ne0$, every position where $v=1$ contributes exactly one to the sum of the two weights; hence the weight is at least $\operatorname{wt}(v)\geq2^{(m-1)-(r-1)}=2^{m-r}$. The monomial $X_1\cdots X_r$ is one exactly on $2^{m-r}$ points, attaining the bound. Degrees greater than $m$ give the same full code; the zero code at negative degree has no nonzero word whose weight could define a finite minimum.

= 4J
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= Solution
{parent=4J}

This is a <generalized linear model> with independent <Bernoulli distributions> for the responses and the <logit link>. Under treatment coding with colour one as the reference level, the <linear predictor> is
$$
\log\frac{p_i}{1-p_i}=\beta_0+\beta_2\mathbf1_{\{c_i=2\}}+\beta_3\mathbf1_{\{c_i=3\}}+\beta_4\mathbf1_{\{c_i=4\}}+\beta_w w_i.
$$
Colour is a <categorical variable>, not a numerical trend. Width has the same slope for every colour, since no <interaction term> is fitted. The parameters are estimated by <maximum likelihood estimation>.

The approximate <Wald confidence interval> for the width coefficient is $\boxed{0.46\pm1.96(0.106)}$, or approximately $(0.2522,0.6678)$. This uses the approximate <normal distribution> of the <maximum likelihood estimator>, rather than a confidence interval for an individual binary response.

For the requested crab, the fitted <linear predictor> is $\widehat\eta=-11.38-0.22+0.46(20)=-2.40$. Applying the inverse <logit link> gives $\boxed{\widehat p=(1+e^{2.40})^{-1}\simeq0.0832}$. The rounded displayed coefficients need not reproduce the reported test statistics exactly.

= 5E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= i
{parent=5e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Put $x=1+y$ and retain linear terms. The <delay differential equation> is $\dot y(t)=-\alpha y(t-T)$, with <characteristic equation of a delay differential equation> $\lambda+\alpha e^{-\lambda T}=0$. Write $z=\lambda T$ and $q=\alpha T$. If a root has $\operatorname{Re}z\geq0$, then $|z|=q e^{-\operatorname{Re}z}\leq q$. Writing $z=a+ib$ gives $a=-q e^{-a}\cos b$. For $q<\pi/2$, $|b|<\pi/2$ and the right side is negative, contradicting $a\geq0$. All characteristic roots therefore lie in the left half-plane.

An imaginary root $z=ib$ requires $\cos b=0$ and $b=q\sin b$. The first crossing is $q=\pi/2$, $z=\pm i\pi/2$. Implicit differentiation gives $dz/dq=-e^{-z}/(1+z)$, whose real part at the positive crossing is $(\pi/2)/(1+\pi^2/4)>0$. The later imaginary crossings occur at $q=\pi/2+2\pi k$ and also enter the right half-plane. Thus the equilibrium is **locally exponentially asymptotically stable when**
$$
\boxed{\alpha T<\pi/2,}
$$
and unstable when $\alpha T>\pi/2$. At equality the <linearized equation> has neutral oscillatory modes; it is the <Hopf bifurcation> threshold. If stability is meant for the nonlinear equation itself, the borderline can also be resolved. In dimensionless time $s=t/T$, let $q_0=\pi/2$. A small center oscillation has the form $y=R\cos\theta+R^2(\cos2\theta/5+\sin2\theta/10)+O(R^3)$. Substitution at quadratic order gives these second-harmonic coefficients. At cubic order the fundamental forcing is $q_0R^3(\cos\theta/20+3\sin\theta/20)$. Projection onto the center mode divides the complex forcing by the characteristic derivative $1+iq_0$, yielding the <Hopf normal form> amplitude equation
$$
\frac{dR}{ds}=-\frac{q_0(3q_0-1)}{20(1+q_0^2)}R^3+O(R^5).
$$
Its cubic coefficient is negative, so the equilibrium at equality is locally asymptotically stable, with algebraic rather than exponential decay. Thus the usual strictly decaying linear condition is $\alpha T<\pi/2$, while **nonlinear local asymptotic stability includes $\alpha T=\pi/2$**. The purely imaginary linear modes alone would not justify that borderline conclusion.

= ii
{parent=5e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The delayed positive term models recruitment depending on the population a time $T$ earlier; the quadratic negative term represents instantaneous density-dependent losses. Thus this <delay differential equation> delays recruitment rather than the strength of crowding acting on the present population.

With $x=1+y$, the <linearized equation> is $\dot y=\alpha y(t-T)-2\alpha y(t)$. Its <characteristic equation of a delay differential equation> is $\lambda+2\alpha=\alpha e^{-\lambda T}$. A root with nonnegative real part would satisfy $|\lambda+2\alpha|\geq2\alpha$, whereas $|\alpha e^{-\lambda T}|\leq\alpha$. This is impossible. Consequently **the equilibrium is locally exponentially asymptotically stable for every $\alpha>0$ and $T>0$**. There is no upper bound on $\alpha T$ in this model.

= 6B
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=6B}

The <Papperitz symbol> describes a homogeneous second-order <Fuchsian differential equation> on the <Riemann sphere>, with three <regular singular points> and the two <indicial exponents> at each. At a finite point $z_j$, an exponent $r$ corresponds locally to a factor $(z-z_j)^r$; at infinity it corresponds to $z^{-r}$. Repeated or resonant exponents can require logarithms. The six exponents obey the <Fuchs relation> that their sum is one. With three singularities there is no independent <accessory parameter>, so these data determine the normalized equation.

A <Möbius transformation of a Papperitz symbol> moves the singular points without changing their exponents. Multiplication of the dependent variable by a local power shifts the two exponents by that power, using the reciprocal local coordinate at infinity.

Set $w=z/(z-1)$ and $v(w)=F(a,c-b,c;w)$. At $w=0,1,\infty$, the exponent pairs for $v$ are respectively $(0,1-c)$, $(0,b-a)$, and $(a,c-b)$. The <Möbius transformation> sends $z=0,1,\infty$ to $w=0,\infty,1$. Before rescaling, $v(w(z))$ therefore has exponent pairs $(0,1-c)$ at zero, $(a,c-b)$ at one, and $(0,b-a)$ at infinity. Multiplication by $(1-z)^{-a}$ subtracts $a$ at one and adds $a$ at infinity. The resulting pairs are
$$
(0,1-c),\quad(0,c-a-b),\quad(a,b),
$$
exactly those of the original <Gauss hypergeometric equation>. Near zero choose the branch with $(1-z)^{-a}=1$ at zero. The resulting solution is analytic there and has value one. Uniqueness of the normalized analytic solution yields
$$
\boxed{F(a,b,c;z)=(1-z)^{-a}F(a,c-b,c;z/(z-1)).}
$$
This is the <Pfaff transformation>. The normalization presupposes the usual admissible parameters, in particular $c\notin\{0,-1,-2,\ldots\}$. Elsewhere the identity holds by <analytic continuation> with compatible branches, or by parameter limits wherever those are defined.

= 7D
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= a
{parent=7d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $x_1,x_2,x_3$ be longitudinal displacements from equilibrium. The original diagram has masses $m,2m,m$, and no attachments to fixed walls. The <Lagrangian> is
$$
L=\tfrac12m(\dot x_1^2+2\dot x_2^2+\dot x_3^2)-\tfrac12k\big[(x_2-x_1)^2+(x_3-x_2)^2\big].
$$
The <Euler-Lagrange equations> give
$$
\boxed{m\ddot x_1=k(x_2-x_1),\quad2m\ddot x_2=k(x_1-2x_2+x_3),\quad m\ddot x_3=k(x_2-x_3).}
$$
The forces sum to zero, consistent with <momentum conservation> for an isolated molecule.

= b
{parent=7d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <normal mode> write $x_j=A_j e^{i\omega t}$. The <generalized eigenvalue problem> is
$$
k\begin{pmatrix}1&-1&0\\-1&2&-1\\0&-1&1\end{pmatrix}A
=\omega^2m\operatorname{diag}(1,2,1)A.
$$
The three independent <eigenvectors> and corresponding squared frequencies are
$$
\boxed{(1,1,1):0;\qquad(1,0,-1):k/m;\qquad(1,-1,1):2k/m.}
$$
The last two have zero mass-weighted displacement and are <orthogonal> in the mass inner product. The lowest frequency is zero: it represents a rigid translation with both springs unchanged. Its general motion includes uniform centre-of-mass velocity, not just a constant displacement. Superpositions of the two oscillatory <normal modes> and this translation give the general solution.

= 8C
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= Solution
{parent=8C}

The <effective number of relativistic energy degrees of freedom> count two photon polarizations, four electron/positron spin states, and six neutrino/antineutrino states, with the <Fermi-Dirac distribution> factor $7/8$ for <fermions>. Treating these species as relativistic and at a common temperature gives $\boxed{g_*=2+(7/8)(4+6)=10.75}$.

The mass-equivalent <radiation mass density> is $\rho=(\pi^2/30)g_*(k_BT)^4/(\hbar^3c^5)$. Therefore the <Friedmann equation> gives
$$
H(T)=\left(\frac{4\pi^3Gg_*}{45\hbar^3c^5}\right)^{1/2}(k_BT)^2.
$$
At $T_0=10^{10}\,\mathrm K$, $H_0\simeq0.503\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$. Equating the specified <weak interaction> rate to $H_0(T/T_0)^2$ gives $(T_*/T_0)^3=H_0/(1\,\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ and hence
$$
\boxed{T_*\simeq7.96\times10^9\,\mathrm K,\qquad T_*\propto g_*^{1/6}.}
$$
The approximate equilibrium <neutron-to-proton ratio>, neglecting chemical potentials, is $r_*\simeq\exp[-(m_n-m_p)c^2/(k_BT_*)]$, about $0.152$ using the mass difference $1.293\,\mathrm{MeV}$. This is a freeze-out estimate rather than a detailed reaction-network computation.

After weak <cosmological weak freeze-out>, free neutrons undergo <beta-minus decay>; neutrinos decouple, and electron-positron annihilation heats the photons relative to the neutrinos. Initially the abundant energetic photons destroy deuterium almost as soon as it forms. Once the <deuterium bottleneck> ends, nuclear reactions rapidly pass through deuterium, tritium and helium-three to <helium-4>. Reactions eventually become too slow compared with <cosmic expansion>; nearly all surviving neutrons are bound in helium-four. Its mass fraction is approximately $Y_4=2r/(1+r)$, where $r$ is evaluated when nuclear assembly begins.

Increasing $g_*$ increases the <Hubble parameter>, raises $T_*$, increases the freeze-out <neutron-to-proton ratio>, and leaves less time for <free-neutron decay after freeze-out>. Both effects increase $Y_4$; decreasing $g_*$ reverses them. Most deuterium is only an intermediate product and is burned into helium, so its residual abundance and its absolute change are tiny compared with the helium mass fraction. **This does not imply a negligible fractional change in residual deuterium.** Faster expansion also shortens the time available to burn it, and its relative abundance can be appreciably sensitive to extra radiation. The distinction matters when interpreting the final wording of the question; joint deuterium and helium abundances constrain extra relativistic species, as discussed in https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.1408[the Particle Data Group nucleosynthesis review].

= 9H
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

The <Chinese remainder theorem> says that for pairwise coprime positive moduli $n_i$, arbitrary residue classes modulo each $n_i$ specify one residue class modulo $\prod_i n_i$. For an odd positive integer $N=\prod p_i^{e_i}$, the <Jacobi symbol> is $(a/N)=\prod_i(a/p_i)^{e_i}$, where each factor is a <Legendre symbol>; for $N=1$ the empty product is one. The <Jacobi symbol> is multiplicative in $a$, and is zero exactly when $a$ and $N$ have a common prime factor.

= i
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**False.** The <Jacobi symbol> $(2/15)=(2/3)(2/5)=(-1)(-1)=1$, yet $2$ is not a <quadratic residue> modulo three. A solution modulo fifteen would give one modulo three, which is impossible. A positive <Jacobi symbol> need not indicate a square modulo a composite modulus.

= ii
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**True.** Since $N$ is not a square, some exponent $e_i$ is odd. Choose a <quadratic nonresidue> modulo that $p_i$ and choose residue one at every other prime. The <Chinese remainder theorem> produces a unit $b$ modulo $N$ with $(b/N)=-1$. Multiplication by $b$ permutes the residue classes modulo $N$. Thus, by multiplicativity of the <Jacobi symbol>,
$$
\sum_{a=1}^N(a/N)=\sum_{a=1}^N(ba/N)=-\sum_{a=1}^N(a/N).
$$
The sum is therefore $\boxed{0}$. Nonunits contribute zero, so the permutation argument covers every term.

= iii
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**False.** The <Carmichael number> $561=3\cdot11\cdot17$ supplies a counterexample. For every $a$ coprime to $561$, the <Fermat little theorem> gives $a^{560}\equiv1$ modulo each of these primes, since $2,10,16$ all divide $560$. The <Chinese remainder theorem> then gives $a^{560}\equiv1\pmod{561}$. Thus the proposed <Fermat primality test> can fail to detect a composite for every coprime base.

= iv
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**True.** First suppose $p^e\mid N$ with $e\geq2$ maximal. By the <Chinese remainder theorem>, choose $a\equiv1+p^{e-1}\pmod{p^e}$ and $a\equiv1$ modulo all other prime-power factors. This is a unit, with <Jacobi symbol> one, because it is one modulo every prime. For $m=(N-1)/2$, the <binomial theorem> gives
$$
a^m\equiv1+mp^{e-1}\not\equiv1\pmod{p^e},
$$
because $p\nmid m$ and all higher terms contain $p^e$. It is therefore a witness to the desired failure.

If $N$ is square-free and composite, it has at least two prime factors. Choose $a$ to be a <quadratic nonresidue> at one prime $p$ and one at every other prime, again using the <Chinese remainder theorem>. Then $(a/N)=-1$. At a different odd prime $q\mid N$, however, $a^m\equiv1\not\equiv-1\pmod q$. These two cases prove that **every odd composite has an Euler-Jacobi witness**, unlike the situation for the ordinary <Fermat primality test>.

= 10J
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= Solution
{parent=10J}

Write $R_j=I-P_{-j}$. Minimizing the residual sum of squares first over the coefficients of $X_{-j}$ reduces the problem to minimizing $\|R_jY-bR_jX_j\|^2$. Full column rank ensures $R_jX_j\ne0$. Thus the <partial regression> formula and its <variance> are
$$
\boxed{\widehat\beta_j=\frac{X_j^TR_jY}{X_j^TR_jX_j},\qquad\operatorname{var}(\widehat\beta_j)=\frac{\sigma^2}{X_j^TR_jX_j}.}
$$
This uses $R_j^T=R_j=R_j^2$ and the isotropic error <covariance matrix> $\sigma^2I$.

Since the span of $X_k$ is contained in the column space of $X_{-j}$, <orthogonal projection> onto the latter removes at least as much squared norm. Consequently
$$
X_j^TR_jX_j\leq\|X_j\|^2-\frac{(X_k^TX_j)^2}{\|X_k\|^2},
$$
and inversion proves the requested lower bound. Full rank makes the denominator positive.

Adding $c\mathbf1_n$ to column $j\geq2$ leaves the column space unchanged. Every old fitted vector is reproduced by keeping all slopes unchanged and replacing the <regression intercept> by $\widehat\beta_1-c\widehat\beta_j$. Uniqueness of the <ordinary least squares estimators> then proves that slope $j$ is unchanged. In particular all columns can be centered by such changes. Applying the previous projection argument to the centered design proves the bound with $X_j-\bar X_j\mathbf1_n$ and $X_k-\bar X_k\mathbf1_n$; the normalized inner product is now the usual <sample correlation>.

Each reported coefficient test is the two-sided <Student's t-test> of $H_0:\beta_j=0$ against $H_1:\beta_j\ne0$, conditional on the other predictors. Under the null, $\widehat\beta_j/(\widehat\sigma\sqrt{(X^TX)^{-1}_{jj}})$ has a <Student's t-distribution> with $100-3=97$ degrees of freedom. At five percent the intercept is significant, but neither individual test slope is. The final <F-test> compares the full model against an intercept-only model: $H_0:\beta_{\rm Maths}=\beta_{\rm Stats}=0$. It has $(2,97)$ degrees of freedom and rejects at five percent, since its $p$-value is $0.003166$. Thus the predictors jointly explain variation, without strong evidence separating their individual conditional effects.

The two test predictors have <sample correlation> $0.9371630$, although each has only moderate correlation with the final exam response. The <variance inflation factor> is $1/(1-0.9371630^2)\simeq8.22$. Their nearly shared direction produces substantial <multicollinearity>, inflating the individual slope standard errors. The joint <F-test> can detect this common predictive direction even though either coefficient, given the other, has a large $p$-value.

= 11E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= a
{parent=11e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a smooth test function $f$, integration by parts in the <Fokker-Planck equation> gives
$$
\frac d{dt}\mathbb E f(x)=\mathbb E\left[A_i\partial_i f+\tfrac12B_{ij}\partial_i\partial_j f\right].
$$
We use the decay and absence of boundary flux intended in the question, sufficiently strong to remove the moment-weighted boundary terms. For $f=x_k$, only $\partial_i f=\delta_{ik}$ survives, giving $d\langle x_k\rangle/dt=\langle A_k\rangle$. For $f=x_kx_l$, the first derivative is $\delta_{ik}x_l+\delta_{il}x_k$ and the second is $\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl}+\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}$. The <Fokker-Planck diffusion matrix> is symmetric, so
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}\langle x_kx_l\rangle=\langle x_lA_k+x_kA_l+B_{kl}\rangle.}
$$
The factor one-half cancels the two equal diffusion contributions, including when $k=l$.

= b
{parent=11e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\xi=x-\langle x\rangle$ and $C_{ij}=\langle\xi_i\xi_j\rangle$. In the <linear noise approximation>, $A_i=a_{ik}\xi_k$ and $B_{ij}=b_{ij}$. At the stationary mean, the preceding moment identity gives $\dot C_{ij}=a_{ik}C_{kj}+a_{jk}C_{ik}+b_{ij}$. Hence the stationary <covariance matrix> solves the <continuous Lyapunov equation>
$$
\boxed{aC+Ca^T+b=0,}
$$
which is precisely the requested component formula because $C$ is symmetric.

= c
{parent=11e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The three jump vectors and their rates are $(1,0)$ at rate $\lambda$, $(-1,1)$ at rate $\gamma x_1$, and $(0,-1)$ at rate $\beta x_2$. Summing rate times jump gives the <Fokker-Planck drift vector>, while summing rate times the outer product of the jump gives the <Fokker-Planck diffusion matrix>:
$$
\boxed{A=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda-\gamma x_1\\\gamma x_1-\beta x_2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda+\gamma x_1&-\gamma x_1\\-\gamma x_1&\gamma x_1+\beta x_2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The maturation event simultaneously decreases larvae and increases adults; this accounts for the negative off-diagonal entries. Since $A$ is affine, its expectation is its value at the mean. Setting both mean derivatives to zero yields $\boxed{\langle x_1\rangle=\lambda/\gamma,\quad\langle x_2\rangle=\lambda/\beta}$.

= d
{parent=11e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

At the stationary mean,
$$
a=\begin{pmatrix}-\gamma&0\\\gamma&-\beta\end{pmatrix},\qquad
b=\lambda\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\-1&2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The $(1,1)$ entry of the <continuous Lyapunov equation> gives $-2\gamma C_{11}+2\lambda=0$, so $C_{11}=\lambda/\gamma$. Its $(1,2)$ entry gives $\gamma C_{11}-(\gamma+\beta)C_{12}-\lambda=0$, hence $C_{12}=0$. Finally $(2,2)$ gives $2\gamma C_{12}-2\beta C_{22}+2\lambda=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{var}(x_1)=\lambda/\gamma,\qquad\operatorname{var}(x_2)=\lambda/\beta,\qquad\operatorname{cov}(x_1,x_2)=0.}
$$
Both variances equal their means; the absence of stationary covariance does not mean that the maturation jumps are independent events in the two populations.

= 12C
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= a
{parent=12c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In a frame centered at the fixed point or centre of mass, a rotating material point has velocity $\dot r=\omega\times r$. The identity $|\omega\times r|^2=|\omega|^2|r|^2-(\omega\cdot r)^2$ converts the <rotational kinetic energy> into $T=\tfrac12\omega_iI_{ij}\omega_j$, where the <inertia tensor> is
$$
\boxed{I_{ij}=\int\rho(r)(|r|^2\delta_{ij}-r_ir_j)\,d^3r.}
$$
Likewise integrating $r\times(\omega\times r)$ gives the <angular momentum> $\boxed{L=I\omega}$. These expressions refer to rotational motion about the chosen origin.

= b
{parent=12c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In <principal axes>, $2E=\sum I_i\omega_i^2$ and $L^2=\sum I_i^2\omega_i^2$. Substitute the <Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body>:
$$
\dot E=\omega_1\omega_2\omega_3[(I_2-I_3)+(I_3-I_1)+(I_1-I_2)]=0.
$$
For the squared <angular momentum>,
$$
\tfrac12\frac d{dt}L^2=\omega_1\omega_2\omega_3[I_1(I_2-I_3)+I_2(I_3-I_1)+I_3(I_1-I_2)]=0.
$$
Thus **both $E$ and $L^2$ are conserved**, even though the body-frame components of $L$ generally change.

= c
{parent=12c}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Place the origin at the centre and the axes along the edges. Symmetry makes the off-diagonal entries of the <inertia tensor> zero. Uniform density gives $\langle x^2\rangle=a^2/12$, $\langle y^2\rangle=b^2/12$, $\langle z^2\rangle=c^2/12$. Consequently the <principal moments of inertia> are
$$
\boxed{I_1=\frac M{12}(b^2+c^2),\quad I_2=\frac M{12}(c^2+a^2),\quad I_3=\frac M{12}(a^2+b^2).}
$$
For example $I_1=\rho\int(y^2+z^2)\,dx\,dy\,dz$ gives the first expression directly.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here $(I_1,I_2,I_3)=(20,17,5)Ma^2/12$, so $I_1>I_2>I_3$ and $e_2$ is the intermediate axis. Eliminating the other components from $L^2=2I_2E$ and the <conservation of energy> relation gives, with $J=2E-I_2\omega_2^2$,
$$
\omega_1^2=\frac{I_2-I_3}{I_1(I_1-I_3)}J,\qquad
\omega_3^2=\frac{I_1-I_2}{I_3(I_1-I_3)}J.
$$
Thus the second <Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body> reduces to
$$
\boxed{\dot\omega_2=\pm\frac35\left(\frac{24E}{17Ma^2}-\omega_2^2\right).}
$$
The sign is minus the sign of $\omega_1\omega_3$; it is constant along a nontrivial branch. Let $\Omega=\sqrt{24E/(17Ma^2)}$. Since $\omega_2(0)=0$, integration yields $\omega_2(t)=\pm\Omega\tanh(3\Omega t/5)$, with the same sign as in the scalar equation. The remaining components are
$$
\omega_1(t)=\omega_1(0)\operatorname{sech}(3\Omega t/5),\qquad
\omega_3(t)=\omega_3(0)\operatorname{sech}(3\Omega t/5).
$$
Their initial squares are fixed by the preceding algebraic formulas with $J=2E$; their initial signs determine the branch. Hence $\boxed{\omega(t)\longrightarrow\pm\Omega e_2}$ as $t\to\infty$. This trajectory is an <intermediate-axis separatrix>: approaching intermediate-axis rotation on this special conserved-energy level does not make that rotation stable to generic perturbations. If $E=0$, all components vanish identically.

= 13I
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Logic and Set Theory}

The <axiom of foundation> states that every nonempty set $A$ has a member $a$ with $a\cap A=\varnothing$. The principle of <epsilon induction> states that a formula $\phi$ satisfying $\forall x[(\forall y\in x\;\phi(y))\Rightarrow\phi(x)]$ holds for every set $x$.

Assume <axiom of foundation> and suppose a progressive formula has a counterexample $a$. In the <transitive closure> of $\{a\}$, use <axiom schema of separation> to collect the counterexamples into a nonempty set $B$. Foundation supplies $b\in B$ having no member in $B$. Since the closure is transitive, every member of $b$ satisfies $\phi$, contradicting progressiveness. Conversely, if a nonempty set $A$ had no foundation-minimal member, every $x\in A$ would have a member $y\in x\cap A$. The formula $x\notin A$ is then progressive: if every member of $x$ lies outside $A$, $x$ cannot belong to $A$. <Epsilon induction> would make $A$ empty, a contradiction. This proves equivalence in the presence of the other <Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory>.

To obtain the <cumulative hierarchy>, perform <epsilon induction> on the assertion that $x$ belongs to some $V_\alpha$. If this holds for each member $y$, <axiom schema of replacement> collects their least such ordinals into a set. Choose an ordinal $\beta$ above them all. Then $x\subseteq V_\beta$ and $x\in V_{\beta+1}$. Thus every set occurs in the hierarchy. Use the <rank of a set> convention $\operatorname{rk}(x)=\sup_{y\in x}(\operatorname{rk}(y)+1)$, equivalently the least $\alpha$ with $x\subseteq V_\alpha$.

= i
{parent=13i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

An <ordinal> has rank equal to itself. Thus the <rank of a set> here is $\sup\{\omega+2,\omega+3,\omega+4\}=\boxed{\omega+4}$.

= ii
{parent=13i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <Kuratowski ordered pair> $(m,n)=\{\{m\},\{m,n\}\}$. Its <rank of a set> is $\max(m,n)+2$. Every pair in the <Cartesian product> has finite rank, but those ranks are unbounded in $\omega$. Therefore $\operatorname{rk}(\omega\times\omega)=\sup_{m,n<\omega}(\max(m,n)+3)=\boxed{\omega}$.

= iii
{parent=13i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Represent a <function> by its graph of <Kuratowski ordered pairs>. For $f:\omega\to\omega^2$,
$$
\operatorname{rk}(f)=\sup_{n<\omega}\big(\max(n,f(n))+3\big)\leq\omega^2.
$$
The bound is attained by $f(n)=\omega n$, which is a <cofinal function> into $\omega^2$. Taking the rank of the set of all such functions adds one at this attained maximum. Thus $\boxed{\operatorname{rk}((\omega^2)^\omega)=\omega^2+1}$. The extra successor is essential: unlike the previous product, this set actually contains a member of rank $\omega^2$.

= 14I
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= Solution
{parent=14I}

A <matching in a graph> from $X$ to $Y$ is a collection of edges with disjoint endpoints covering every vertex of $X$; equivalently it chooses a distinct neighbour in $Y$ for each $x\in X$. For a finite <bipartite graph>, the <Hall marriage theorem> says this exists exactly when $|N(A)|\geq|A|$ for every $A\subseteq X$. Necessity follows by counting the distinct matched neighbours.

For sufficiency, induct on $|X|$. If a nonempty proper subset $A$ is tight, $|N(A)|=|A|$, Hall's condition holds on $(A,N(A))$. It also holds on $(X\setminus A,Y\setminus N(A))$: for $B\subseteq X\setminus A$, the original condition applied to $A\cup B$ gives $|N(B)\setminus N(A)|\geq|B|$. Induction provides two disjoint <matching in a graph> whose union covers $X$. If no nonempty proper subset is tight, choose an edge $xy$, possible by Hall's condition. Delete its endpoints. Each nonempty subset of $X\setminus\{x\}$ had at least one extra neighbour, so deleting $y$ leaves Hall's condition intact. Induction and then the edge $xy$ finish the proof. The single-vertex case is immediate.

For the degree condition, fix $A\subseteq X$ and double-count with weights:
$$
|N(A)|\geq\sum_{y\in N(A)}\frac{d_A(y)}{d(y)}
=\sum_{x\in A}\sum_{y\sim x}\frac1{d(y)}
\geq\sum_{x\in A}\frac{d(x)}{d(x)}=|A|.
$$
Every denominator is nonzero; the last inequality uses $d(y)\leq d(x)$ along each edge. The <Hall marriage theorem> therefore proves **the required matching exists**. Finiteness, as in the usual course version of the theorem, is understood; the same proof covers a finite $X$ with arbitrary $Y$ and finite degrees.

= 15F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= a
{parent=15f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A one-dimensional continuous <group representation> is a continuous <group homomorphism> $\rho:S^1\to\mathbb C^\times$. Its image is compact. If some $|\rho(z)|$ differed from one, its positive or negative powers would be unbounded, so the image lies in $S^1$. Lift $t\mapsto\rho(e^{it})$ to a continuous real argument $a(t)$ with $a(0)=0$. Then $a(s+t)-a(s)-a(t)$ is a continuous integer multiple of $2\pi$ and vanishes at $(0,0)$, hence is always zero. The continuous <Cauchy functional equation> gives $a(t)=ct$. Periodicity forces $e^{2\pi ic}=1$, so $c=n\in\mathbb Z$. Consequently $\boxed{\rho(z)=z^n}$; conversely every such map is a continuous one-dimensional <group representation>.

= b
{parent=15f}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <character of a representation> is $\chi_V(g)=\operatorname{tr}\rho_V(g)$. Average any positive inner product over the compact group using <Haar measure> to obtain a <unitary representation>. On the <maximal torus> $\operatorname{diag}(z,z^{-1})$, the commuting unitary operators admit a simultaneous eigenbasis. Each eigenline carries a circle <character of a representation> $z^n$ by part (a). Hence
$$
\boxed{\chi_V(z)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}m_nz^n,\qquad m_n\in\mathbb N_0,}
$$
with finitely many nonzero coefficients. Every element of <special unitary group> is conjugate to an element of this torus, so this describes the full <character of a representation> via its torus restriction. Here the coefficient notation includes zero multiplicities.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The matrix $w=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}\in\mathrm{SU}(2)$ conjugates $\operatorname{diag}(z,z^{-1})$ to $\operatorname{diag}(z^{-1},z)$. A <character of a representation> is invariant under <conjugation>, since the trace is invariant under similarity. Therefore $\boxed{\chi_V(z)=\chi_V(z^{-1})}$, and opposite torus weights have equal multiplicities.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write $V_r=\operatorname{Sym}^r\mathbb C^2$ for the irreducible <SU(2) representation> of dimension $r+1$, with <character of a representation> $z^r+z^{r-2}+\cdots+z^{-r}$. Here $V=V_3$, with weights $3,1,-1,-3$. Squaring its <character of a representation> and successively removing the highest-weight irreducible characters gives
$$
\boxed{V\otimes V\cong V_6\oplus V_4\oplus V_2\oplus V_0.}
$$
The dimensions $7+5+3+1=16$ check the decomposition; complete reducibility follows from the invariant inner product.

For the <exterior square>, add distinct pairs of weights. Its <character of a representation> is $z^4+z^2+2+z^{-2}+z^{-4}=\chi_{V_4}+\chi_{V_0}$. Equivalently it is $(\chi_V(z)^2-\chi_V(z^2))/2$. Hence $\boxed{\Lambda^2V\cong V_4\oplus V_0}$, of dimensions five and one. The complementary <symmetric square> is $V_6\oplus V_2$.

= 16H
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Number Fields}

= Solution
{parent=16H}

For a <number field> $K$, let $\mathcal O_K$ be its <ring of integers of a number field>, $r_1$ the number of real embeddings and $r_2$ the number of conjugate pairs of nonreal complex embeddings, so $[K:\mathbb Q]=r_1+2r_2$. The <Dirichlet unit theorem> says
$$
\mathcal O_K^\times\cong\mu(K)\times\mathbb Z^{r_1+r_2-1},
$$
where $\mu(K)$ is the finite group of <roots of unity> in $K$. Thus an imaginary <quadratic number field> has only roots of unity as units: usually $\{\pm1\}$, with four for $d=-1$ and six for $d=-3$. A real <quadratic number field> has units $\pm\epsilon^n$ for a <fundamental unit> $\epsilon>1$ and integers $n$.

For $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{26})$, $\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z[\sqrt{26}]$ and the <field norm> is $x^2-26y^2$. The element $\epsilon=5+\sqrt{26}$ has norm $-1$ and is a unit. It is the smallest positive unit greater than one. Indeed for $1<u<\epsilon$, its conjugate is $\pm1/u$, so the coefficient $y=(u\mp1/u)/(2\sqrt{26})$ is a positive integer less than two. Therefore $y=1$, and $x^2=26\pm1$ forces $x=5$, giving $u=\epsilon$, a contradiction. The <Dirichlet unit theorem> now gives $\boxed{\mathcal O_K^\times=\{\pm(5+\sqrt{26})^n:n\in\mathbb Z\}}$. Its least positive norm-one generator is $\epsilon^2=51+10\sqrt{26}$.

For the <generalized Pell equation>, write $\alpha=x+y\sqrt{26}$ with norm $N=\pm10$. Change the sign if necessary and multiply by a power of $\epsilon$ to place it in $\sqrt{10}\leq\alpha<\epsilon\sqrt{10}$. These operations preserve integrality and the absolute norm, though the latter operation can change the norm's sign. Since $\alpha'=N/\alpha$, we have $x=(\alpha+N/\alpha)/2\geq0$ and $y=(\alpha-N/\alpha)/(2\sqrt{26})\geq0$. The upper bound gives $y<4$. Checking $y=0,1,2,3$ in $x^2=26y^2\pm10$ leaves only $(x,y)=(4,1),(6,1)$. Both reduced elements lie in the chosen interval. Therefore **all integral solutions of either sign are**
$$
\boxed{x+y\sqrt{26}=\pm(4+\sqrt{26})(5+\sqrt{26})^n\quad\text{or}\quad\pm(6+\sqrt{26})(5+\sqrt{26})^n,\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Negative powers are integral because $\epsilon^{-1}=\sqrt{26}-5$. In the first family the norm is $-10(-1)^n$ and in the second it is $10(-1)^n$, so this also separates the two equations. The reduction proves exhaustion rather than merely generating examples.

= 17F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= i
{parent=17f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Here one must distinguish two conventions for a <radical extension>. The solvability theorem says that a finite extension with solvable normal-closure <Galois group> is contained in a tower of radical adjunctions. Under the convention that this containment defines a radical extension, this is the requested assertion.

Let $N/K$ be the finite normal closure and let $m$ be divisible by the orders of its <Galois group> elements. Adjoin a primitive $m$th <root of unity>, obtaining $K'=K(\zeta_m)$, which itself is a radical adjunction since $\zeta_m^m=1$. The <Galois group> of $NK'/K'$ embeds in the solvable group of $N/K$, so it is solvable. A composition series with cyclic prime-order quotients gives a tower of cyclic prime-degree extensions above $K'$. Each is a radical adjunction, as the following argument shows.

For a cyclic extension $E/F$ of prime degree $p$ containing $\zeta_p$, let $\sigma$ generate its <Galois group>. The $F$-linear operator $\sigma$ satisfies $X^p-1$, a product of distinct linear factors in characteristic zero. Thus it is diagonalizable. Because $\sigma\ne1$, it has an eigenvector $a\ne0$ with eigenvalue $\zeta_p^j\ne1$. Then $a^p\in F$ and $a\notin F$, so $E=F(a)$ by primeness of the degree. Applying this at every step produces a radical tower containing $L$.

If “radical extension” instead means that $L/K$ itself must be such a tower, the printed statement is false without this qualification. For example the real cubic subfield of $\mathbb Q(\zeta_7)$ is cyclic and solvable, but contains no nonrational $a$ with $a^n\in\mathbb Q$: all three conjugates are real roots of the same equation $X^n=a^n$, which has at most two real roots, while any nonrational element of the cubic field has three distinct conjugates. Consequently no nontrivial radical adjunction can start a tower inside it. **Containment in a radical tower is the valid general conclusion.**

= ii
{parent=17f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take the <Vandermonde determinant> $\Delta=\prod_{i<j}(x_i-x_j)$. Its square is a <symmetric polynomial>, hence a polynomial in the <elementary symmetric polynomials> and belongs to $K$. It is nonzero because the variables are algebraically independent. A transposition of two variables fixes every $e_i$ and therefore fixes $K$, but sends $\Delta$ to $-\Delta\ne\Delta$ in characteristic zero. Thus $\boxed{\alpha=\Delta\notin K,\quad\alpha^2\in K}$.

= iii
{parent=17f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In characteristic two, let $K=\mathbb F_2(t)$ and $L=K(u)$ with $u^2+u=t$. The <Artin-Schreier polynomial> is irreducible: if $r\in K$ solved it, a finite pole of $r$ would create a pole of even order in $r^2+r$, impossible for $t$; at infinity a pole of $r$ likewise gives even order, while $t$ has order one. A constant $r$ cannot solve it either. Its derivative is one, so this degree-two extension is <separable field extension>. Adjoining $\sqrt\alpha$ in characteristic two is either trivial or <purely inseparable field extension>, since the derivative of $X^2-\alpha$ vanishes. Hence $\boxed{L\ne K(\sqrt\alpha)\text{ for every }\alpha\in K}$.

For the second example take $\boxed{K=\mathbb F_{11}}$. Every extension still has characteristic eleven, and $X^{11}-1=(X-1)^{11}$ in it. Thus the only eleventh <root of unity> is one, whose order is one, and there is no primitive eleventh root in any extension.

= 18H
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= Solution
{parent=18H}

The <Mayer-Vietoris sequence> for $K=M\cup N$ is the <long exact sequence in homology>
$$
\cdots\to H_n(M\cap N)\xrightarrow{(i_*,-j_*)}H_n(M)\oplus H_n(N)\xrightarrow{k_*+l_*}H_n(K)\xrightarrow{\partial_n}H_{n-1}(M\cap N)\to\cdots.
$$
We use integer coefficients. A <simplicial cycle> $c$ in $K$ can be written $c=m+n$ with chains in $M,N$. Since $\partial m=-\partial n$, this chain lies in $M\cap N$ and is a cycle. Define $\partial_n[c]=[\partial m]$; changing the splitting or the cycle representative changes this by a boundary, giving a well-defined <connecting homomorphism>.

First consider two acyclic subcomplexes. Their intersection is either empty or acyclic. If empty, positive-degree <homology> of their disjoint union vanishes. If nonempty, the reduced <Mayer-Vietoris sequence> implies the same conclusion, including degree one since the intersection has zero reduced $H_0$. This proves the claim for two subcomplexes.

Inductively let $U=M_1\cup\cdots\cup M_{n-1}$, $V=M_n$, and $W=U\cap V=\bigcup_{i<n}(M_i\cap M_n)$. Discard empty members as necessary. Both $U$ and $W$ satisfy the same intersection hypothesis with at most $n-1$ members, so their homology vanishes in degrees at least $n-2$. The exact segment $H_i(U)\oplus H_i(V)\to H_i(K)\to H_{i-1}(W)$ then gives $\boxed{H_i(K)=0\text{ for }i\geq n-1}$. For $n\geq3$ these degrees are positive and the induction applies directly; empty $W$ again means a disjoint union.

For sharpness, take the boundary of an $(n-1)$-simplex, covered by its $n$ facets. Every nonempty proper intersection of facets is a simplex and thus has the <homology of a point>; the intersection of all facets is empty. The union is a <sphere> $S^{n-2}$ and $H_{n-2}$ is nonzero. For $n=2$ it is two isolated vertices and $H_0\cong\mathbb Z^2$; for $n\geq3$, $H_{n-2}\cong\mathbb Z$. **The degree bound cannot be lowered.**

= 19G
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=19G}

Use an <inner product> linear in the first variable. For each $y$, the bounded linear functional $x\mapsto\langle Tx,y\rangle$ is represented uniquely by a vector $T^*y$ under the <Riesz representation theorem>, so $\langle Tx,y\rangle=\langle x,T^*y\rangle$. Uniqueness and the conjugate-linearity in the second argument imply $T^*(ay+bz)=aT^*y+bT^*z$. Furthermore
$$
\|T^*y\|=\sup_{\|x\|=1}|\langle Tx,y\rangle|\leq\|T\|\|y\|.
$$
Thus the <adjoint operator> exists, is linear and bounded, and is unique because a vector orthogonal to every $x$ is zero.

A <normal operator> satisfies $T^*T=TT^*$. The unilateral <unilateral shift operator> $S(x_1,x_2,\ldots)=(0,x_1,x_2,\ldots)$ on $\ell^2$ is bounded but not normal: $S^*S=I$, whereas $SS^*=I-P_{e_1}$. For general $T$,
$$
\|Tx\|^2-\|T^*x\|^2=\langle(T^*T-TT^*)x,x\rangle.
$$
Normality implies equality of norms. Conversely equality for every $x$ makes this self-adjoint operator's quadratic form zero; the <polarization identity> then gives $T^*T-TT^*=0$.

An <approximate eigenvalue> $\lambda$ has unit vectors $x_n$ with $\|(T-\lambda I)x_n\|\to0$. It is necessarily in the <spectrum>, since a bounded inverse would prevent this. Conversely suppose $\lambda$ is not an approximate eigenvalue. Then $A=T-\lambda I$ is bounded below, $\|Ax\|\geq c\|x\|$. It is injective and has closed range: a convergent sequence $Ax_n$ forces $x_n$ to be Cauchy. The operator $A$ is normal, so $\|A^*x\|=\|Ax\|$ and $\ker A^*=0$. The identity $(\operatorname{ran}A)^\perp=\ker A^*$ makes its range dense. Closed and dense means surjective, and its inverse is bounded by $1/c$. Hence $\lambda$ is in the resolvent. This proves $\boxed{\sigma(T)=\sigma_{\rm ap}(T)}$.

= 20F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Geometry}

= i
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a smooth projective <algebraic curve>, a <linear system of divisors> comes from a finite-dimensional space $V\subseteq H^0(C,\mathcal O(D))$. A basis of sections $s_0,\ldots,s_n$ defines $f(P)=[s_0(P):\cdots:s_n(P)]$, using any local trivialization; a common scalar change does not alter the projective point. A <base-point-free linear system> makes this defined everywhere, and $f^*\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^n}(1)\cong\mathcal O(D)$. Pulling back a hyperplane gives the zero divisor of a linear combination of the sections, an element of the original system.

A system with base divisor $B$ can instead define a map after removing $B$, but its hyperplane pullbacks have class $D-B$. Thus base-point-freeness is needed for the stated original-system property. For a <closed immersion>, the system must also separate distinct points and tangent directions; equivalently it is a <very ample linear system>. On a smooth curve the latter tangent condition is separation of the length-two subscheme at each point.

= ii
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Riemann-Roch theorem> states, for a divisor $D$ on a smooth projective curve of genus $g$,
$$
\boxed{\ell(D)-\ell(K_C-D)=\deg D+1-g,}
$$
where $\ell(D)=\dim H^0(C,\mathcal O(D))$ and $K_C$ is a <canonical divisor>, of degree $2g-2$. Negative-degree divisors have no nonzero global sections. The theorem's usual formulation here is over an algebraically closed field, as in the curve course.

= iii
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Here $\deg K_C=2$. For $D$, $D-P$ and $D-P-Q$, including $Q=P$, the degrees are $5,4,3$, all greater than two. Thus the correction term in the <Riemann-Roch theorem> vanishes and
$$
\ell(D)=4,\qquad\ell(D-P)=3,\qquad\ell(D-P-Q)=2.
$$
The first drop supplies a section nonzero at every chosen point, proving <base-point-freeness of a linear system>. For distinct $P,Q$, the second drop supplies a section vanishing at one but not the other, proving point separation. For $Q=P$, it supplies a section vanishing to order exactly one, proving tangent separation. Hence the complete <linear system of divisors> is <very ample> and $\boxed{C\hookrightarrow\mathbb P^3}$ is the induced <closed immersion>.

= 21G
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= Solution
{parent=21G}

Regard complex matrices as a real vector space of dimension $2n^2$ and Hermitian matrices as a real vector space of dimension $n^2$. Set $F(A)=AA^*$. Its derivative is $DF_A(H)=HA^*+AH^*$. At a unitary $A$, any Hermitian $S$ is reached by $H=SA/2$. Hence $I$ is a <regular value>, and the <regular level set theorem> makes $\mathrm U(n)=F^{-1}(I)$ a smooth real <manifold> of dimension $\boxed{n^2}$.

The <tangent space> is the kernel of this derivative. Equivalently,
$$
\boxed{H\in T_A\mathrm U(n)\iff A^*H+H^*A=0\iff H=AK\text{ with }K^*=-K.}
$$
At $I$, therefore, $H$ is skew-Hermitian. The explicit <geodesic> with initial point $I$ and velocity $H$ is $\gamma(t)=e^{tH}$. The <matrix exponential> is unitary because $e^{tH^*}e^{tH}=I$, and $\dot\gamma(0)=H$. Its acceleration is $\gamma H^2$. Since $H^2$ is Hermitian and every tangent vector at $\gamma$ is $\gamma K$ with $K$ skew-Hermitian, the induced real inner product satisfies
$$
\langle\gamma H^2,\gamma K\rangle=\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}(H^2K^*)=0.
$$
Indeed the trace of a Hermitian matrix times a skew-Hermitian matrix is purely imaginary. Its acceleration is therefore always normal to the manifold, which is the <geodesic equation> for an induced Euclidean metric. Diagonalizing $H=W\operatorname{diag}(i\lambda_j)W^*$ gives the equivalent explicit construction $W\operatorname{diag}(e^{it\lambda_j})W^*$.

= 22J
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= a
{parent=22j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For nonnegative measurable functions $f_n$ on a <measure space>, the <Fatou lemma> states
$$
\boxed{\int\liminf_n f_n\,d\mu\leq\liminf_n\int f_n\,d\mu.}
$$
In particular $\mathbb E[\liminf X_n]\leq\liminf\mathbb E X_n$ for nonnegative <random variables>; infinite values are allowed. Nonnegativity, or a common integrable lower bound after subtraction, is essential.

= b
{parent=22j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Weak convergence> $X_k\Rightarrow X$ means that $\mathbb E f(X_k)\to\mathbb E f(X)$ for every bounded continuous real function $f$ on $\mathbb R^d$; it is a statement about their distributions, not their coupling.

The <central limit theorem> says that for independent identically distributed real variables of finite mean $\mu$ and variance $\sigma^2$, $\sqrt n(\bar X_n-\mu)\Rightarrow N(0,\sigma^2)$. For $\sigma>0$ this is equivalent to convergence of $(\sum X_j-n\mu)/(\sigma\sqrt n)$ to the standard <normal distribution>. For $\sigma=0$, every variable equals $\mu$ almost surely and the limit is the point mass at zero.

For the proof when $\sigma>0$, put $Y=(X-\mu)/\sigma$. Its <characteristic function> has expansion $\varphi_Y(t)=1-t^2/2+o(t^2)$. To justify this using only a second moment, subtract the first two Taylor terms of $e^{itY}$ and use <dominated convergence theorem>, with the second-order remainder bounded by a constant times $t^2Y^2$. Independence gives the characteristic function of the normalized sum as $[\varphi_Y(t/\sqrt n)]^n\to e^{-t^2/2}$. The <Levy continuity theorem> says that pointwise convergence of characteristic functions to a characteristic function continuous at zero implies <weak convergence>. Since $e^{-t^2/2}$ is the characteristic function of $N(0,1)$, the result follows.

= c
{parent=22j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Since $\varphi(0)=1$ and $\varphi(h)+\varphi(-h)=2\mathbb E\cos(hX)$, the expression in the hypothesis equals $\mathbb E Z_n$, with $Z_n=2[1-\cos(h_nX)]/h_n^2\geq0$. For every finite real $X$, the Taylor expansion of cosine gives $Z_n\to X^2$. The <Fatou lemma> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E X^2\leq\liminf_n\frac{2\varphi(0)-\varphi(-h_n)-\varphi(h_n)}{h_n^2}<\infty.}
$$
No differentiability of the <characteristic function> or prior integrability of $X^2$ was assumed; the nonnegative difference quotient establishes it.

= 23K
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability}

= i
{parent=23k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume $h(x)>0$, so the conditioning event has positive probability. For a continuous-time <Markov chain>, write $\tau_y=\int_0^{T_A}\mathbf1_{\{X_s=y\}}\,ds$. At each time $s<T_A$, the <Markov property> gives $\mathbb P(T_A<\infty\mid\mathcal F_s)=h(X_s)$. Using <Tonelli theorem> for the nonnegative occupation integral,
$$
\mathbb E_x[\tau_y\mathbf1_{\{T_A<\infty\}}]
=\int_0^\infty\mathbb E_x[\mathbf1_{\{s<T_A,X_s=y\}}h(y)]\,ds
=h(y)\mathbb E_x\tau_y.
$$
Division by $h(x)$ yields $\boxed{\mathbb E_x[\tau_y\mid T_A<\infty]=[h(y)/h(x)]\mathbb E_x\tau_y}$. The discrete-time proof replaces the integral by a sum. Infinite expectations are allowed in the nonnegative sense; if $h(y)=0$, the numerator is zero directly, avoiding an ambiguous product of zero and infinity.

= ii
{parent=23k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In the neutral <Moran model>, a fixed population of $N$ individuals carries either allele $a$ or its alternative. Each individual reproduces at rate one and replaces a uniformly selected individual. If $i$ carry $a$, the two changing-state rates are $q_{i,i+1}=q_{i,i-1}=i(N-i)/N$. States zero and $N$ are absorbing. This time normalization is essential for the stated expected time.

The hitting probability $h_i$ of $N$ before zero satisfies $h_{i+1}-2h_i+h_{i-1}=0$, with $h_0=0,h_N=1$. Thus $\boxed{h_i=i/N}$. Let $G_{ij}$ be expected occupation time at $j$ before absorption. The <Green function> equation in its starting index is
$$
\frac{i(N-i)}N(G_{i+1,j}-2G_{ij}+G_{i-1,j})=-\mathbf1_{\{i=j\}},\qquad G_{0j}=G_{Nj}=0.
$$
It is linear on either side of $j$. Matching values and the difference jump at $j$ gives
$$
G_{ij}=\begin{cases}i/j,&i\leq j,\\(N-i)/(N-j),&i\geq j.\end{cases}
$$
For example the difference jump is $-N/[j(N-j)]$, so multiplying by $j(N-j)/N$ gives exactly $-1$.

Condition on fixation using part (i), with $A=\{N\}$ and the occupation stopped on absorption at either endpoint; on paths absorbed at zero, hitting $N$ is impossible. Equivalently apply the Markov-property occupation proof directly to this event. The conditional occupation time at $j$ is $(j/i)G_{ij}$. For $j\geq i$ it is one; for $j<i$ it is $[(N-i)/i]j/(N-j)$. Summing over all transient states yields
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E_i[\tau\mid\text{fixation}]=N-i+\frac{N-i}{i}\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}\frac{j}{N-j}.}
$$
Here $1\leq i\leq N$; at $i=N$ the fixation time is zero, while at $i=0$ conditioning is undefined.

= 24J
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= Solution
{parent=24J}

The nonparametric <bootstrap sample> is a sample of size $n$ drawn independently with replacement from the <empirical distribution> $\widehat F_n=n^{-1}\sum_i\delta_{X_i}$, conditional on the observed data. To simulate it, take independent uniform $U_j$ and set $X_j^b=X_{1+\lfloor nU_j\rfloor}$, assigning $U_j=1$ to the last index if it occurs in a finite-precision generator.

Let $G_n^b$ be the conditional <cumulative distribution function> of $\sqrt n(\bar X_n^b-\bar X_n)$, and let $q_n(p)=\inf\{t:G_n^b(t)\geq p\}$. A basic <bootstrap confidence interval> is
$$
\boxed{C_n=[\bar X_n-q_n(1-\alpha/2)/\sqrt n,\ \bar X_n-q_n(\alpha/2)/\sqrt n].}
$$
In practice the conditional quantiles are estimated from many simulated bootstrap means; the asymptotic argument below describes exact bootstrap quantiles or a simulation count tending to infinity.

For the required uniform-convergence fact, given $\epsilon>0$ choose $a<b$ with $F(a)<\epsilon$ and $1-F(b)<\epsilon$. By continuity, choose a finite mesh on $[a,b]$ with successive increases of $F$ less than $\epsilon$. Pointwise convergence at the mesh points makes $|F_n-F|<\epsilon$ there for large $n$. Monotonicity sandwiches both functions between their neighbouring mesh values, so $|F_n(t)-F(t)|<2\epsilon$ throughout $[a,b]$. The same monotonicity and the chosen tail bounds control the two exterior rays by $2\epsilon$. Hence $\sup_t|F_n(t)-F(t)|\to0$.

The <bootstrap consistency theorem for the sample mean> states that, for iid observations with $0<\sigma^2<\infty$, the conditional law of $\sqrt n(\bar X_n^b-\bar X_n)$ converges weakly, in probability, to $N(0,\sigma^2)$. By continuity of that limiting distribution and the preceding monotonicity argument, the convergence of conditional distribution functions is uniform in probability. Since the limit is strictly increasing, $q_n(p)\to\sigma\Phi^{-1}(p)$ in probability for $0<p<1$. The coverage event is
$$
q_n(\alpha/2)\leq\sqrt n(\bar X_n-\mu)\leq q_n(1-\alpha/2).
$$
The <central limit theorem>, quantile convergence and <Slutsky theorem> therefore give $\boxed{\mathbb P^{\mathbb N}(\mu\in C_n)\to1-\alpha}$.

**Positive variance is needed for this exact coverage conclusion.** If the finite variance allowed in the wording is zero, all observations equal $\mu$ almost surely and the interval collapses to $\{\mu\}$, with coverage one. The bootstrap distribution is still consistent with the degenerate limit, but a nonrandomized usual interval cannot then have limiting coverage $1-\alpha$ for $0<\alpha<1$.

= 25K
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Optimization and Control}

= Solution
{parent=25K}

Treat the <white noise> as innovations with conditional mean zero and conditional second moment $v$, independent of the chosen past control. This is the convention needed for the <dynamic programming> calculation. The printed stage cost is $\tfrac12x^2+u^2$, not one-half of both terms.

With terminal value $V_0(x)=x^2$, induction in the <Bellman equation> gives
$$
V_h(x)=\min_u\{\tfrac12x^2+u^2+\mathbb E V_{h-1}(x+u+\epsilon)\}.
$$
If $V_{h-1}(z)=z^2+(h-1)v$, completing the square yields
$$
\tfrac12x^2+u^2+(x+u)^2+hv=x^2+2(u+x/2)^2+hv.
$$
Thus $\boxed{V_h(x)=x^2+hv,\quad u^*(x)=-x/2}$ for every horizon, in particular $V_6(x_0)=x_0^2+6v$. The same completion gives the average-cost <Bellman equation> solution $\boxed{\phi(x)=x^2,\quad\lambda=v}$, up to an additive constant in $\phi$.

For every policy in $P$, conditional second moments satisfy $\mathbb E[x_{t+1}^2\mid\mathcal F_t]=(x_t+u_t)^2+v\leq0.9x_t^2+v$. Iterating gives
$$
\mathbb E_\pi x_t^2\leq0.9^t x_0^2+v\sum_{j=0}^{t-1}0.9^j\leq\boxed{x_0^2+10v}.
$$
The Bellman inequality implies $\mathbb E_\pi\sum_{t<h}(\tfrac12x_t^2+u_t^2)\geq hv+x_0^2-\mathbb E_\pi x_h^2$. Dividing by $h$ and using the bound proves that every admissible policy has lower limiting average cost at least $v$, even if its ordinary limit does not exist. The feedback $u=-x/2$ belongs to $P$ because $(x+u)^2=x^2/4\leq0.9x^2$. It makes the Bellman inequality an equality, and its bounded terminal second moment proves its average cost tends to $\boxed{v}$. Therefore it is optimal over $P$.

Merely uncorrelated noise with the displayed unconditional moments would not ensure the conditional identities for arbitrary adapted controls. The innovation interpretation specifies the hypothesis used in the calculation.

= 26K
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= i
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the buyer's <utility indifference price>: $\pi(Y)$ is the cash payment satisfying
$$
\sup_{X\in V}\mathbb E U(w_0+X)
=\sup_{X\in V}\mathbb E U(w_0-\pi(Y)+X+Y).
$$
Assume the utilities are well-defined, the finite indifference price exists, and the suprema are attained as allowed. Write the common reference value as $M$. For claims $Y_1,Y_2$, choose optimizers $X_1,X_2$ at their indifference prices $p_1,p_2$. Because $V$ is a <vector space>, $X=\theta X_1+(1-\theta)X_2$ is admissible. The <concavity> of $U$ gives
$$
\mathbb E U\big(w_0-[\theta p_1+(1-\theta)p_2]+X+\theta Y_1+(1-\theta)Y_2\big)\geq\theta M+(1-\theta)M=M.
$$
The optimized utility decreases with the payment; strict increase of $U$ and attainment make the comparison strict whenever a smaller payment is substituted into an attained optimizer. Hence the payment restoring utility $M$ is at least $\theta p_1+(1-\theta)p_2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\pi(\theta Y_1+(1-\theta)Y_2)\geq\theta\pi(Y_1)+(1-\theta)\pi(Y_2).}
$$
The sign corresponds to buying the payoff. A seller's price uses a different convention.

= ii
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here $\alpha>0$ and $\gamma_j>0$. The asset payoff has a centered <Laplace distribution>. Its <moment generating function> is $\mathbb E e^{tX}=\alpha^2/(\alpha^2-t^2)$ for $|t|<\alpha$, and diverges otherwise. Expected <constant absolute risk aversion utility> for a holding $\theta$ is
$$
-e^{-\gamma_jw_0}\,e^{\gamma_jp\theta}\frac{\alpha^2}{\alpha^2-\gamma_j^2\theta^2}.
$$
To maximize it, minimize the logarithm of the positive factor on $|\gamma_j\theta|<\alpha$. Its derivative is $\gamma_jp+2\gamma_j^2\theta/(\alpha^2-\gamma_j^2\theta^2)$, and its second derivative is strictly positive. It diverges at the endpoints, so the unique critical point is the global optimizer:
$$
\boxed{\theta_j=-\frac{\sqrt{1+p^2\alpha^2}-1}{\gamma_jp}.}
$$
At $p=0$ use the continuous limit $\theta_j=0$. The other quadratic root is outside the finite-utility domain.

Let $\Gamma^{-1}=\sum_j\gamma_j^{-1}$. Aggregate demand is $D(p)=-(\sqrt{1+p^2\alpha^2}-1)/(\Gamma p)$. Unit positive supply requires $p<0$ and $\sqrt{1+p^2\alpha^2}=1-\Gamma p$. Squaring and excluding $p=0$ gives
$$
\boxed{p=-\frac{2\Gamma}{\alpha^2-\Gamma^2}\quad(\alpha>\Gamma).}
$$
This negative price satisfies the unsquared equation and every individual finite-utility constraint. When $\alpha\leq\Gamma$, there is **no finite clearing price**. Indeed $D(p)<\alpha/\Gamma$ for every finite negative $p$, with limit $\alpha/\Gamma$ as $p\to-\infty$. If $\alpha<\Gamma$ this maximum limiting demand is below supply; if equal, demand only approaches one at an infinitely negative price. A formal positive solution obtained by squaring when $\alpha<\Gamma$ violates the original demand equation.

= 27C
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Asymptotic Methods}

= Solution
{parent=27C}

Take the logarithmic derivative of the proposed <asymptotic expansion>. Through order $z^{-1}$, $u'/u=\lambda+\mu/z+O(z^{-2})$ and $u''/u=\lambda^2+2\lambda\mu/z+O(z^{-2})$. Matching the constant and inverse-power terms gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda^2+f_0\lambda+g_0=0,\qquad\mu=-\frac{f_1\lambda+g_1}{2\lambda+f_0}.}
$$
The second formula assumes a simple root, $2\lambda+f_0\ne0$. At a repeated root the ansatz can fail: the next equation first requires $f_1\lambda+g_1=0$, and if that holds higher orders determine the remaining behaviour.

For the <Bessel differential equation>, $\lambda=\pm i$ and $\mu=-1/2$. Set $u=e^{\lambda z}z^{-1/2}v(z)$. Direct substitution leaves $v''+2\lambda v'+(1/4-\nu^2)z^{-2}v=0$. With $v=1+a/z+O(z^{-2})$, matching $z^{-2}$ gives $a=(1/4-\nu^2)/(2\lambda)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{a^{(1)}=\frac{i(4\nu^2-1)}8,\qquad a^{(2)}=-\frac{i(4\nu^2-1)}8.}
$$
Two constant multiples of the <Hankel functions> normalize their leading coefficients to one and realize these solutions in their appropriate sectors.

For generic $\nu$, these are sectorial expansions, not unchanged expansions for a single branch over arbitrarily continued $\arg z$. The factors $z^{-1/2}$ require a branch, and crossing sector boundaries can introduce the other exponential by <Stokes phenomenon> and analytic continuation. More precisely, the standard normalized first Hankel solution has its expansion for $-\pi+\delta\leq\arg z\leq2\pi-\delta$, and the second for $-2\pi+\delta\leq\arg z\leq\pi-\delta$, with any fixed positive $\delta$ inside these sectors. In particular both are valid in the common slit sector $-\pi<\arg z<\pi$, away from its boundaries. These sector statements are recorded in https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.17[NIST's Hankel asymptotics]. Exceptional half-integer orders have terminating elementary expressions, so a blanket assertion of nonzero Stokes mixing for every order would be too strong.

= 28B
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= Solution
{parent=28B}

A <horseshoe for an interval map> consists of two branches covering a common interval: there are an open interval $J$ and disjoint open subintervals $K_0,K_1\subseteq J$ with $f(K_0)=f(K_1)=J$. Equivalently one can work with two closed intervals with disjoint interiors whose images cover their union, and then restrict the branches. <Glendinning chaos> means that some positive iterate $f^m$ has such a horseshoe, rather than requiring one for $f$ itself.

For the three-cycle, use the <intermediate value theorem> on endpoint images. The <interval covering relations> are $I_a\to I_b$ and $I_b\to I_a,I_b$. Consequently both $f^2(I_a)$ and $f^2(I_b)$ cover $I_a\cup I_b$. This proves **$f^2$ has a horseshoe**. The <directed covering graph of an interval map> has exactly these forced arrows; a closed walk $a\to b\to b\to a$ has length three and a primitive itinerary. Pulling back the intervals successively and using the fixed-point property produces a point fixed by $f^3$ with this itinerary. It cannot be fixed by $f$, and a shared endpoint belongs to the already given three-cycle, so a genuine period-three orbit results.

For the increasing spatial order of the five-cycle, let $I_j=[x_{j-1},x_j]$, $1\leq j\leq4$. The forced graph is
$$
I_1\to I_2\to I_3\to I_4,\qquad I_4\to I_1,I_2,I_3,I_4.
$$
The primitive loops $I_4$, $I_3I_4$, $I_2I_3I_4$, and $I_1I_2I_3I_4$ force exact periods $\boxed{1,2,3,4}$. For periods below five the known five-cycle endpoints cannot be responsible, so the interval itineraries give distinct interior orbits. Both $g^2(I_3)$ and $g^2(I_4)$ cover $I_3\cup I_4$, so **$g$ is guaranteed to be Glendinning-chaotic**.

A horseshoe for $g$ itself is not guaranteed. As a counterexample put $x_j=j$ on $[0,4]$ and interpolate by $g(x)=x+1$ for $0\leq x\leq3$, $g(x)=16-4x$ for $3\leq x\leq4$. This has the required five-cycle. The unique fixed point is $16/5$, with $g(x)>x$ to its left and $g(x)<x$ to its right. Any nondegenerate closed interval whose image covers itself must contain this fixed point in its interior: an interval entirely on either side, or with the fixed point as an endpoint, fails to cover its extreme endpoint. Two intervals with disjoint interiors therefore cannot both cover themselves, as a horseshoe would require. Hence this example has no one-iterate horseshoe.

For the alternative spatial order, set $y_0=x_4,y_1=x_2,y_2=x_1,y_3=x_3,y_4=x_0$ and $J_j=[y_{j-1},y_j]$. Endpoint images give
$$
J_1\to J_4,\quad J_2\to J_2,J_3,\quad J_3\to J_1,\quad J_4\to J_1,J_2.
$$
The self-loop at $J_2$, the two-cycle $J_1J_4$, and the four-cycle $J_2J_3J_1J_4$ force $\boxed{1,2,4}$; period three is not guaranteed. To see this last claim, take the connect-the-dots map at $y_j=j$ with values $(4,3,1,0,2)$. Its exact interval transitions are those displayed. Any length-three closed itinerary is the repeated self-loop in $J_2$; there the map is the affine decreasing function $5-2x$, whose third iterate has only its original fixed point. Endpoints belong to the five-cycle. Thus no exact three-cycle exists in this example.

Chaos is still guaranteed: from $J_2$ there are two different length-four closed walks, four self-loops and $J_2J_3J_1J_4J_2$. Pullbacks give disjoint branches of $g^4$ over a common interval in $J_2$, hence a <horseshoe for an interval map> for an iterate. A horseshoe for $g$ itself is again not guaranteed: the same connect-the-dots example is strictly decreasing on $[0,3]$ and increasing on $[3,4]$ with image $[0,2]$. Its unique fixed point is $5/3$, again with $g(x)>x$ to the left and $g(x)<x$ to the right. The same fixed-point argument rules out two self-covering intervals with disjoint interiors. These counterexamples distinguish the three separate questions about periods, chaos and a one-iterate horseshoe.

= 29E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Partial Differential Equations}

= a
{parent=29e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For smooth periodic initial data, Fourier expansion reduces the equation to $\dot{\widehat u}(m,t)=-(1+m^2)\widehat u(m,t)$. Therefore define the <Fourier multiplier> solution operators by
$$
\widehat{S(t)u_0}(m)=e^{-(1+m^2)t}\widehat u_0(m).
$$
Their <Sobolev norm> satisfies $\|S(t)u_0\|_{H^s}^2\leq e^{-2t}\|u_0\|_{H^s}^2\leq\|u_0\|_{H^s}^2$, so they extend to <contractive linear operators> on $H^s_{\rm per}$. Multiplication of the Fourier factors gives $S(t+s)=S(t)S(s)$ and $S(0)=I$. Finally
$$
\|S(t)u-u\|_{H^s}^2=\sum_m(1+m^2)^s|e^{-(1+m^2)t}-1|^2|\widehat u(m)|^2\to0
$$
by <dominated convergence theorem>, dominated by four times the summable norm terms. This proves a **strongly continuous contraction semigroup** for every requested $s$; the semigroup property extends continuity from zero to other times. For smooth data, termwise derivatives converge and recover the original <Cauchy problem for a partial differential equation> uniquely.

= b
{parent=29e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $v=e^{t/2}u$. The cancellation of the damping and mass terms gives $v_{tt}=v_{xx}$, with $v(x,0)=u_0(x)$ and $v_t(x,0)=u_1(x)+u_0(x)/2$. For the <wave equation>, $w_+=v_t+v_x$ satisfies $(\partial_t-\partial_x)w_+=0$, and $w_-=v_t-v_x$ satisfies $(\partial_t+\partial_x)w_-=0$. Thus these combinations are transported on straight <characteristics>. Integrating them and matching the initial displacement yields the <d'Alembert formula>, and hence
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=e^{-t/2}\left[\frac{u_0(x+t)+u_0(x-t)}2+\frac12\int_{x-t}^{x+t}\left(u_1(y)+\frac12u_0(y)\right)dy\right].}
$$
Here the initial functions are extended periodically to the real line. Direct differentiation verifies both initial conditions and the equation, and arbitrary repeated differentiation shows the solution is smooth and periodic.

This characteristic derivation also proves <finite propagation speed> from first principles: the value at $(x,t)$ depends only on the two endpoint displacements and initial velocity data in $[x-t,x+t]$. If two data sets agree on that interval, their solutions agree at that point. Equivalently initially supported data have no influence outside the unit-speed characteristic cone, interpreted on the periodic covering line. Multiplication by $e^{-t/2}$ does not enlarge this domain of dependence.

= 30A
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=30A}

The <interaction picture> removes the evolution generated by $H_0$: $|\psi_I(t)\rangle=e^{iH_0t/\hbar}|\psi_S(t)\rangle$. Differentiating the <Schrodinger equation> gives $i\hbar\partial_t|\psi_I\rangle=\lambda V_I(t)|\psi_I\rangle$, with $V_I=e^{iH_0t/\hbar}V(t)e^{-iH_0t/\hbar}$. The integral equation, iterated once, gives
$$
|\psi_I(t)\rangle=|\chi\rangle-\frac{i\lambda}{\hbar}\int_0^t V_I(s)|\chi\rangle\,ds+O(\lambda^2).
$$
Distinct-energy <eigenstates> are orthogonal. Their transition amplitude is therefore $-i\lambda\hbar^{-1}\int_0^t\langle\phi|V(s)|\chi\rangle e^{i(E'-E)s/\hbar}ds+O(\lambda^2)$. Squaring its modulus gives
$$
\boxed{P_{\chi\to\phi}(t)=\frac{\lambda^2}{\hbar^2}\left|\int_0^t\langle\phi|V(s)|\chi\rangle e^{i(E'-E)s/\hbar}ds\right|^2+O(\lambda^3).}
$$
The first-order amplitude produces a second-order probability.

For the isotropic <quantum harmonic oscillator>, parity makes $\int x_i x_3|\chi|^2d^3x=0$ for $i=1,2$. Rotational symmetry and the given radial integral give $\int x_3^2|\chi|^2d^3x=\hbar/(2m\omega)$. Thus $\langle\phi_i|\hat x_3|\chi\rangle=\delta_{i3}\sqrt{\hbar/(2m\omega)}$, and the energy gap is $\hbar\omega$. The time integral is $\int_0^\infty e^{(-\mu+i\omega)s}ds=(\mu-i\omega)^{-1}$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{P_{\chi\to\phi_3}=\frac{\lambda^2}{2\hbar m\omega(\mu^2+\omega^2)}+O(\lambda^3),\qquad P_{\chi\to\phi_1}=P_{\chi\to\phi_2}=0\text{ to this order}.}
$$
The perturbation picks the excitation polarized along the third coordinate, rather than an arbitrary member of the degenerate first excited level.

= 31A
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Applications of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=31A}

The <reciprocal lattice> is $\Lambda^*=\{G:G\cdot\ell\in2\pi\mathbb Z\text{ for every }\ell\in\Lambda\}$. With oriented primitive-cell volume $v_c=a_1\cdot(a_2\times a_3)$, its basis is $b_1=2\pi(a_2\times a_3)/v_c$ and the cyclic variants; thus $b_i\cdot a_j=2\pi\delta_{ij}$.

The total potential is $V(x)=\sum_\ell U(x-\ell)$. Using the <Fourier transform> convention $\widetilde U(q)=\int e^{-iq\cdot x}U(x)d^3x$, translation gives $\widetilde V(q)=\widetilde U(q)\Delta(q)$, with
$$
\Delta(q)=\prod_{j=1}^3\sum_{n=0}^{N_j-1}e^{-in q\cdot a_j}.
$$
The given <Born approximation> therefore supplies the amplitude $\boxed{-m\Delta(q)\widetilde U(q)/(2\pi\hbar^2)}$. A finite <geometric series> gives, when the denominators are nonzero,
$$
\boxed{|\Delta(q)|=\prod_{j=1}^3\left|\frac{\sin(N_jq\cdot a_j/2)}{\sin(q\cdot a_j/2)}\right|.}
$$
At reciprocal-lattice vectors each phase is one, so $|\Delta|=N_1N_2N_3$, the number of atoms. For large finite crystals these narrow peaks represent coherent <Bragg scattering>, with peak intensity proportional to the squared number of atoms; their finite width reflects the finite crystal size.

For the specified <face-centered cubic lattice>, $v_c=a^3/4$ and
$$
b_1=\frac{2\pi}a(-\hat x+\hat y+\hat z),\quad b_2=\frac{2\pi}a(\hat x-\hat y+\hat z),\quad b_3=\frac{2\pi}a(\hat x+\hat y-\hat z).
$$
The <reciprocal lattice> have coordinates $(2\pi/a)(h,k,l)$ with $h,k,l$ all even or all odd. The two shortest nonzero length families are $G_1=2\pi\sqrt3/a$ for $(111)$ and $G_2=4\pi/a$ for $(200)$. Elastic scattering satisfies $|q|=2|k|\sin(\theta/2)$. The assumed incident magnitude exceeds $G_2/2$, so both families are kinematically accessible, and
$$
\boxed{\frac{\sin(\theta_1/2)}{\sin(\theta_2/2)}=\frac{G_1}{G_2}=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.}
$$
This is the angular prediction for suitable crystal orientations, or for a powder containing those orientations. A fixed single-crystal orientation must additionally satisfy the vector <Elastic Bragg scattering condition> $2k\cdot G+|G|^2=0$; the magnitude bound alone cannot guarantee both peaks for every incident direction.

= 32C
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Physics}

= i
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the <canonical partition function> $Z=\sum_{\{s\}}e^{-\beta E}$, differentiation at fixed temperature gives $\partial_B\log Z=\beta\langle\sum_i s_i\rangle$. Thus the average spin per particle, or <magnetization> in these units, is $\boxed{m=(N\beta)^{-1}\partial_B\log Z}$.

= ii
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $s_i=m+\delta_i$ and neglect the fluctuation product $\delta_i\delta_j$ in the <mean-field approximation>. Then $s_is_j\simeq m(s_i+s_j)-m^2$. There are $ND$ neighbour pairs, and each spin enters $2D$ pairs. The mean-field energy is
$$
E_{\rm MF}=-(B+2DJm)\sum_i s_i+NDJm^2.
$$
The spins now factorize, giving
$$
\boxed{B_{\rm eff}=B+2DJm,\quad Z_1(x)=2\cosh x,\quad C=e^{-\beta NDJm^2},\quad Z=C[2\cosh(\beta B_{\rm eff})]^N.}
$$
The constant corrects the double counting in the interaction; omitting it would give an incorrect mean-field <free energy>.

= iii
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The single-spin average in the effective field is $\tanh(\beta B_{\rm eff})$. Requiring it to equal the assumed average gives the <mean-field self-consistency equation>
$$
\boxed{m=\tanh[\beta(B+2DJm)].}
$$
At $B=0$, the slope of its right side at the origin is $2DJ/(k_BT)$. Thus $\boxed{T_c=2DJ/k_B}$. For $T>T_c$, $m=0$ is the only solution and is the stable minimum: thermal fluctuations dominate. For $T<T_c$, two stable nonzero solutions $\pm m(T)$ represent <spontaneous magnetization>, while the zero solution is unstable. At $T_c$ they merge continuously. Expanding $\tanh x=x-x^3/3+\cdots$ near the transition yields $m^2\sim3(T_c-T)/T_c$, so the mean-field <critical exponent> is one-half. These are predictions of the approximation, not a proof of the exact Ising transition in every dimension.

= iv
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The proposed <Landau free energy> has stationary equation $m[a(T-T_c)+bm^2]=0$. For $T>T_c$ its minimum is $m=0$. For $T<T_c$ the minima are $\boxed{m=\pm\sqrt{a(T_c-T)/b}}$, while zero becomes a maximum; at $T_c$ the positive quartic term keeps the free energy bounded below. Thus the two symmetry-related states, continuous transition and square-root onset agree with the <mean-field approximation>.

More explicitly the mean-field entropy expansion gives $F=F_0+N[k_B(T-T_c)m^2/2+k_BT m^4/12+O(m^6)]$ at zero field. Near $T_c$, replacing $T$ by $T_c$ in the quartic coefficient gives the positive constants $a=Nk_B$ and $b=Nk_BT_c/3$, and reproduces the preceding square-root amplitude. The polynomial is a local expansion near the transition, not a global expression at every temperature.

= 33A
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Electrodynamics}

= Solution
{parent=33A}

Use the <Minkowski metric> with signature $(-,+,+,+)$. The source point is the intersection of the worldline with the observation event's past <light cone>: $R^\mu R_\mu=0$ and $R^0>0$. In components this is $c(t-t_r)=|x-y(t_r)|$ with $t_r<t$. For a timelike worldline the retarded solution is unique in the usual complete-source setting.

Writing $R=|x-y(t_r)|$, $n=(x-y)/R$ and $u^\mu=\gamma(c,v)$ gives $R\cdot u=-\gamma cR(1-n\cdot v/c)$. Thus the <Lienard-Wiechert potentials> are $\phi=q\mu_0c^2/[4\pi R(1-n\cdot v/c)]$ and $A=q\mu_0v/[4\pi R(1-n\cdot v/c)]$. At leading order in $1/r$ and first order in $v/c$, set $R\simeq r,n\simeq\hat r$ and expand the denominator:
$$
\phi\simeq\frac{q\mu_0c}{4\pi r}(c+\hat r\cdot v(t_r)),\qquad A\simeq\frac{q\mu_0}{4\pi r}v(t_r).
$$

Now use $t_-=t-r/c$ and let $a=\dot v(t_-)$. Derivatives of $1/r$ and $\hat r$ give near-field terms of order $r^{-2}$; the radiation terms come from $\partial_t t_-=1$ and $\nabla t_-=-\hat r/c$. The <electric field> and <magnetic field> are therefore
$$
\boxed{E=\frac{q\mu_0}{4\pi r}[\hat r(\hat r\cdot a)-a],\qquad B=-\frac{q\mu_0}{4\pi cr}\hat r\times a=\frac1c\hat r\times E.}
$$
They are transverse to the observation direction. The <Poynting vector> is
$$
\boxed{\frac1{\mu_0}E\times B=\frac{q^2\mu_0}{16\pi^2cr^2}\hat r\,|\hat r\times a|^2.}
$$
Integrating over a sphere uses $\int\sin^2\theta\,d\Omega=8\pi/3$, yielding the nonrelativistic <Larmor formula> $P=q^2\mu_0|a|^2/(6\pi c)$. For <simple harmonic motion>, $|a|^2=A^2\omega^4\cos^2(\omega t_-)$, whose period average is $A^2\omega^4/2$. Multiplication by the period $2\pi/\omega$ gives
$$
\boxed{E_{\rm period}=q^2\mu_0A^2\omega^3/(6c).}
$$
This is the leading radiation energy in the nonrelativistic and far-field approximation.

= 34D
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=General Relativity}

= a
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $f=1-r^2/a^2$ and $r_*=a\operatorname{artanh}(r/a)$. Since $dr_*/dr=f^{-1}$, $dt=du+dr/f$. Substitution gives the outgoing <Outgoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates>
$$
\boxed{ds^2=-f\,du^2-2\,du\,dr+r^2d\Omega^2.}
$$
The radial block has determinant $-1$, independent of $f$, and all coefficients are smooth at $r=a$. This is a <coordinate singularity> of the static chart, not a spacetime singularity. The transformation was defined inside the horizon, but the new metric itself extends smoothly across it.

At $r=0$ the spherical angular coordinates degenerate as usual. Cartesian spatial coordinates make the original static metric regular there: $f=1+O(r^2)$ and the extra spatial radial correction is $x_ix_j/[a^2(1-r^2/a^2)]$. Thus **the origin is not a spacetime singularity either**.

= b
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The inverse radial block is $\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\-1&f\end{pmatrix}$. Since $u_{,a}$ has only its $u$ component nonzero, $g^{ab}u_{,a}u_{,b}=g^{uu}=0$. Therefore $\boxed{\nabla u=-\partial_r\text{ is null}}$; its integral curves lie on the outgoing null hypersurfaces of constant $u$.

= c
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Radial null curves obey $-f\,du^2-2\,du\,dr=0$. For their unparameterized paths,
$$
\boxed{\frac{du}{dr}=0\quad\text{or}\quad\frac{du}{dr}=-\frac2f.}
$$
Inside the horizon, the first family satisfies $dt/dr=1/f$, so $dr/dt=f>0$ and is outgoing. The second satisfies $dt/dr=-1/f$ and is ingoing. Integrating gives $u=u_0$ for outgoing rays and $u=C-2a\operatorname{artanh}(r/a)$ for ingoing rays. The null paths are geodesics after suitable reparameterization; for example in the two-dimensional radial metric each null direction has covariant acceleration proportional to itself.

To show constant-$u$ rays at the requested angle, the diagram uses vertical plotting coordinate $u+r$ and horizontal $r$, with equal scale. This is the stated tilted $u$ grid: constant $u$ becomes a line of slope one. The ingoing curves bend to decreasing $u+r$ as $r$ approaches the horizon.

\Image[paper-4-de-sitter-rays.png]
{height=600}
{title=Outgoing and ingoing radial null rays in de Sitter space on a tilted retarded-time grid}

= d
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For a radial timelike <geodesic>, the static Killing energy is $E=f\dot t$ and normalization gives $\dot r^2=E^2-f$. Choose $E=\sqrt2$ and start at $r(0)=0$ with outward velocity one. An explicit trajectory is
$$
\boxed{r(\tau)=a\sinh(\tau/a).}
$$
It solves $\dot r^2=1+r^2/a^2$ and reaches $r=a$ at $\boxed{\tau_H=a\operatorname{arsinh}(1)<\infty}$. Differentiating the energy equation gives $\ddot r=r/a^2$, the radial geodesic equation, so this is an actual freely falling trajectory.

The regular coordinate satisfies $\dot u=(E-\dot r)/f=1/(E+\dot r)$, which is finite and positive at the crossing. Together with $u(\tau)=u(0)+\int_0^\tau [\sqrt2+\cosh(s/a)]^{-1}ds$, the trajectory extends through the horizon in the regular chart. Divergence of the static time there does not imply infinite <proper time>.

= 35E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics II}

= Solution
{parent=35E}

To recover the printed dispersion relation, interpret the surface maintained flat as an imposed flat upper boundary for the perturbation, so its normal velocity is zero. This is the <rigid-lid approximation>. Work with perturbation <velocity potentials> $\phi_-=A e^{ky}e^{ikx+\sigma t}$ below the interface and $\phi_+=B\cosh[k(y-h)]e^{ikx+\sigma t}$ in the upper layer. They solve the <Laplace equation>, decay as $y\to-\infty$, and obey $\partial_y\phi_+(h)=0$.

The linear <kinematic boundary conditions> at the interface give $(\sigma+ikU)\eta_0=kA$ and $\sigma\eta_0=-kB\sinh(kh)$. Equality of pressures, using the linear <Bernoulli equation>, gives $(\sigma+ikU)A=\sigma B\cosh(kh)$. The equal fluid densities cancel the interface gravitational restoring term. Eliminating $A,B,\eta_0$ yields
$$
\boxed{(\sigma+ikU)^2+\sigma^2\coth(kh)=0.}
$$
With $q=\tanh(kh)$, the roots are
$$
\boxed{\sigma=\frac{Uk}{1+q}(-iq\pm\sqrt q),\qquad\operatorname{Re}\sigma=\pm\frac{Uk\sqrt{\tanh(kh)}}{1+\tanh(kh)}.}
$$
For every $k>0$ one root has positive real part. Thus the <vortex sheet> has a <Kelvin-Helmholtz instability> at every wavelength in this model. If $kh\gg1$, $q\to1$ and the growing rate is $Uk/2$. If $kh\ll1$, $q\sim kh$ and it is $Uk\sqrt{kh}$.

There is a physical qualification to the wording. A dynamically deformable <free surface> at finite gravity is not exactly a flat lid. Its linear conditions combine to $\sigma^2\phi_+(h)+g\partial_y\phi_+(h)=0$. Writing the upper potential as $B\cosh(ky)+C\sinh(ky)$ and using the same interface conditions instead gives
$$
(\sigma+ikU)^2[\sigma^2+gk\tanh(kh)]+\sigma^2[\sigma^2\tanh(kh)+gk]=0.
$$
The stated quadratic follows in the strong-gravity flat-surface limit, or with the flat condition imposed exactly. **It is not the exact dispersion relation of a freely moving upper surface at arbitrary finite $g$.** This makes explicit the boundary interpretation needed for the requested formula.

= 36B
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Waves}

Linearizing the <shallow-water equations> about depth $h$ at rest gives wave speed $c=\sqrt{gh}$. Replacing $h$ by $c$ in the nonlinear equations yields $c_t+u c_x+(c/2)u_x=0$ and $u_t+u u_x+2c c_x=0$. Direct addition and subtraction give
$$
[\partial_t+(u\pm c)\partial_x]\,[u\pm2(c-c_0)]=0.
$$
Thus the <Riemann invariants> $J_\pm=u\pm2(c-c_0)$ are constant on the <characteristics> $C_\pm$ with speeds $u\pm c$. In this problem $c_0=\sqrt{gh_0}$.

= i
{parent=36b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Before a signal reaches the wall, the portion to the right of the leading characteristic $x=c_0t$ is unaffected. Its initial depth and velocity persist, giving $\boxed{u=0,\qquad c=c_0\quad(c_0t\leq x\leq a)}$ for $0\leq t\leq a/c_0$.

= ii
{parent=36b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The release produces a <rarefaction wave> expanding into the dry region. Every incoming $C_-$ characteristic connects to the undisturbed pool, where $J_-=0$, so throughout the simple wave $u=2(c-c_0)$. The outgoing $C_+$ rays emanate from $(0,0)$ and have $x/t=u+c=3c-2c_0$. Solving gives
$$
\boxed{c=\frac13(x/t+2c_0),\qquad u=\frac23(x/t-c_0),\qquad -2c_0t\leq x\leq c_0t.}
$$
The depth is $h=c^2/g$. At the right edge these formulas match the stationary pool; at the left edge $c=0$ and the fluid front travels at $-2c_0$. Beyond it the bed is dry, so a fluid velocity need not be assigned. Values at $t=0$ are understood as one-sided limits of the fan.

The first wall interaction occurs at $t_0=a/c_0$. The leftmost reflected signal follows a $C_-$ ray through $(a,t_0)$. Until a point has encountered this signal it remains in the original fan. In that fan its speed is $u-c=x/(3t)-4c_0/3$. Thus
$$
\dot x-\frac{x}{3t}=-\frac{4c_0}{3},\qquad x(t)=Ct^{1/3}-2c_0t.
$$
Matching at $(a,t_0)$ gives the reflected front
$$
\boxed{x_f(t)=3a(c_0t/a)^{1/3}-2c_0t.}
$$
Consequently the same rarefaction formula remains valid for $-2c_0t\leq x\leq x_f(t)$ when $t\geq t_0$. Between $x_f(t)$ and the wall, information from the wall changes $J_-$ and the simple-wave relation no longer holds. In particular continuing the old formula to $x=a$ would give a negative velocity for $t>t_0$, violating the no-penetration condition $u(a,t)=0$. The reflected region must accommodate both characteristic families; it is not another untouched part of the original fan.

\Image[paper-4-dam-release.png]
{height=600}
{title=Shallow-water dam release characteristics and the first signal reflected from the wall}

= 37E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= a
{parent=37e}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Krylov subspace> is $K_m(A,v)=\operatorname{span}\{v,Av,\ldots,A^{m-1}v\}$. Let $s$ be the first index for which $A^s v$ depends linearly on $v,\ldots,A^{s-1}v$. Such an index exists with $1\leq s\leq n$, since $v\ne0$ and any $n+1$ vectors are dependent. The first $s$ vectors are independent. The relation for $A^sv$ shows that their span is invariant under $A$: multiplying any generator by $A$ stays in that span. Repeated multiplication makes every higher power stay there too. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\dim K_m=m\ (m\leq s),\qquad\dim K_m=s\ (m>s).}
$$
Once a <Krylov subspace> stops growing, it cannot resume growth.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let the distinct <eigenvalues> be $\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_{s'}$. Distinct-eigenvalue <eigenvectors> are independent. A relation $\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}a_kA^kv=0$ becomes $\sum_i c_ip(\lambda_i)w_i=0$, with $p(z)=\sum_{k<m}a_kz^k$. Since every $c_i\ne0$, it requires $p(\lambda_i)=0$ for every $i$. No nonzero polynomial of degree below $s'$ has that many distinct roots. Thus the first $s'$ powers are independent, while $\prod_i(A-\lambda_iI)v=0$ gives the next dependence. Hence $\boxed{s=s'}$. Equivalently the coefficients of these powers form a full-rank <Vandermonde matrix> through this index.

= b
{parent=37e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <linear-system residual> at an approximate solution $x_m$ is $r_m=b-Ax_m$. For symmetric positive definite $A$, the <conjugate gradient method> has $x_m\in x_0+K_m(A,r_0)$ and minimizes the squared $A$-norm of the error over that affine space; its residual is orthogonal to $K_m(A,r_0)$.

If $A$ has $t$ distinct eigenvalues, interpolate $q(\lambda)=1/\lambda$ on them with a polynomial of degree at most $t-1$. The <finite-dimensional spectral theorem> gives $A^{-1}=q(A)$, so the exact correction $x_*-x_0=A^{-1}r_0$ lies in $K_t(A,r_0)$. The minimum possible error at step $t$ is therefore zero. Thus $\boxed{\text{CG terminates in at most }t\text{ steps}}$ in exact arithmetic, possibly earlier if the initial residual has fewer eigencomponents. If $r_0=0$, it terminates immediately.

A symmetric positive definite <matrix preconditioner> $M$ changes the system to $M^{-1/2}AM^{-1/2}z=M^{-1/2}b$, with $x=M^{-1/2}z$; practical preconditioned CG applies solves with $M$. It can accelerate convergence by reducing the <spectral condition number of a positive-definite matrix>, which improves the usual bound involving $(\sqrt\kappa-1)/(\sqrt\kappa+1)$, and by clustering <eigenvalues>, which allows low-degree residual polynomials small on the spectrum even when the extreme-eigenvalue bound is pessimistic.

Choosing $\boxed{M=A}$ makes the transformed matrix $I$, so one step suffices. But applying $M^{-1}$ already requires solving the original problem, and constructing an exact factorization generally incurs its original computational cost. It is therefore not a useful shortcut for a single unsolved system, though an available factorization can be reused for multiple right-hand sides. Effective <matrix preconditioning> balances spectral improvement against inexpensive application.
